            <ArticleTitle>The use of nonpharmacological methods for children's postoperative pain relief: Singapore nurses' perspectives.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>27-38</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1744-6155.2010.00268.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to examine nurses' use of nonpharmacological methods for school-age children's postoperative pain relief.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A survey was conducted in 2008 with a convenience sample of 134 registered nurses from 7 pediatric wards in Singapore.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nurses who were younger, had less education, lower designation, less working experience, and no children of their own used nonpharmacological methods less frequently.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Nurses need training and education on nonpharmacological pain relief methods, particularly on methods that have been shown to be effective in prior research but that were less often used by nurses in this study: massage, thermal regulation, imagery, and positive reinforcement.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2010, Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
